  <h1>The GLP-1 Diet: What to Eat (and Avoid) for Best Results</h1>
  <div class="article-meta"><span>Feb 20, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>14 min read</span></div>
  
<p>Here's the paradox of GLP-1 medications: your appetite is dramatically reduced, but your nutritional needs haven't changed — and in some ways, they've actually increased. When you're eating 40–60% less food than before, every meal needs to work harder. Getting this right is the difference between losing weight healthily and losing weight in a way that leaves you depleted, weak, and nutritionally deficient.</p>
<p>This guide covers the principles, the priorities, and the practical strategies for eating well on a GLP-1 medication.</p>

<h2>The protein priority</h2>
<p>If there's one nutritional message to take from this entire article, it's this: protein first, always. Research consistently shows that 20–40% of weight lost on GLP-1 medications can be lean muscle mass rather than fat. The primary defense against this is adequate protein intake combined with resistance training.</p>
<p>The target for most GLP-1 patients is 0.7–1.0 grams of protein per pound of your ideal body weight per day. For many people, that means 80–120g of protein daily. When your appetite is suppressed, hitting that number requires intentionality.</p>

<h3>Practical protein strategies</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Eat protein first</strong> — At every meal, start with your protein source before touching anything else. If you get full before finishing, at least you've prioritised the most important macronutrient.</li>
  <li><strong>Front-load your day</strong> — Many GLP-1 patients find their appetite is lowest in the evening. Get the bulk of your protein in at breakfast and lunch when you can still tolerate eating.</li>
  <li><strong>Use protein supplements strategically</strong> — A high-quality protein shake (30–40g protein) can fill gaps on days when eating solid food feels impossible. Look for options that are low in fat and sugar to minimise GI irritation.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose dense sources</strong> — Greek yoghurt, cottage cheese, eggs, chicken breast, turkey mince, tinned tuna, and whey protein are all high in protein relative to volume.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout"><strong>The 30g rule:</strong> Research suggests your body can optimally utilise about 30–40g of protein per meal for muscle protein synthesis. Rather than trying to get all your protein in one sitting, aim for 3–4 meals or snacks each containing 25–35g of protein.</div>

<h2>Foods to prioritise</h2>
<p>Beyond protein, focus on nutrient density — foods that deliver maximum vitamins, minerals, and fibre per calorie.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lean proteins:</strong> chicken, turkey, fish, eggs, Greek yoghurt, cottage cheese, tofu, lentils</li>
  <li><strong>Non-starchy vegetables:</strong> leafy greens, broccoli, cauliflower, courgettes, peppers, tomatoes</li>
  <li><strong>Complex carbohydrates:</strong> sweet potatoes, quinoa, brown rice, oats (in moderate portions)</li>
  <li><strong>Healthy fats:</strong> avocado, olive oil, nuts, seeds (in small amounts — fat can slow gastric emptying further and trigger nausea)</li>
  <li><strong>Hydrating foods:</strong> soups, smoothies, watermelon, cucumber — especially helpful when solid food is hard to manage</li>
</ul>

<h2>Foods to avoid (or limit)</h2>
<p>Certain foods are particularly problematic on GLP-1 medications — not because they're "bad" in general, but because they interact poorly with the drug's effects on your digestive system.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>High-fat, greasy foods:</strong> Fried foods, heavy cream sauces, and fatty meats dramatically slow an already-slow stomach and are the number one nausea trigger.</li>
  <li><strong>Very sugary foods:</strong> Sweets, fizzy drinks, and desserts can cause "dumping syndrome" — rapid blood sugar fluctuations that cause nausea, sweating, and dizziness.</li>
  <li><strong>Carbonated drinks:</strong> The gas from sparkling water and fizzy drinks can cause significant bloating and discomfort with slowed gastric emptying.</li>
  <li><strong>Large portions of anything:</strong> Even healthy food will make you miserable if you eat too much. Your capacity is genuinely reduced.</li>
  <li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> Many patients report dramatically reduced alcohol tolerance. GLP-1 medications slow alcohol absorption, intensifying its effects. Start very slowly and cautiously.</li>
</ul>

