<h2>A sample day of eating</h2>
<p>Here's what a good day might look like on a GLP-1 medication, targeting approximately 1,200–1,400 calories and 100g+ protein:</p>
<p><strong>Breakfast (7:30 AM):</strong> Two scrambled eggs with smoked salmon on half a slice of wholemeal toast. Greek yoghurt with a handful of berries. ~35g protein, ~350 calories.</p>
<p><strong>Lunch (12:30 PM):</strong> Chicken and vegetable soup with lentils. Small side salad with olive oil dressing. ~30g protein, ~350 calories.</p>
<p><strong>Afternoon snack (3:30 PM):</strong> Protein shake blended with frozen banana and peanut butter. ~30g protein, ~250 calories.</p>
<p><strong>Dinner (6:30 PM):</strong> Grilled white fish with roasted vegetables and a small portion of quinoa. ~25g protein, ~300 calories.</p>
<p><strong>Daily total:</strong> ~120g protein, ~1,250 calories.</p>

<h2>Supplements to consider</h2>
<p>When you're eating significantly less, certain nutritional gaps become nearly inevitable. Discuss these with your provider:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Multivitamin</strong> — A good quality daily multivitamin covers basic gaps.</li>
  <li><strong>Vitamin D</strong> — Commonly deficient, especially in northern climates.</li>
  <li><strong>Iron</strong> — Particularly for women, as reduced food intake can make iron deficiency more likely.</li>
  <li><strong>B12</strong> — May be reduced with lower food intake and altered gut function.</li>
  <li><strong>Fibre supplement</strong> — Constipation is common on GLP-1s. A psyllium husk supplement can help.</li>
  <li><strong>Biotin</strong> — May help with hair thinning associated with rapid weight loss.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The mindset shift</h2>
<p>Perhaps the biggest adjustment isn't about specific foods — it's about reframing your relationship with eating. On a GLP-1, eating becomes less about pleasure and emotional comfort and more about fuel and nutrition. This can feel like a loss at first, but most patients eventually describe it as liberating.</p>
<p>You're not on a diet. You're on a medication that has changed your hunger signals, and you're choosing to fuel your body well within that new reality. That's a fundamentally different — and much healthier — framework than any diet you've tried before.</p>

